# Plasma Mail

<p align="center">
  <img src="./logo.svg" alt="Plasma Mail logo" width="480" />
</p>

Plasma Mail is a KDE-inspired IMAP mail client designed for focused reading, efficient account management, and desktop integration. It follows a clean, configurable workflow that can be extended with UI, personalization, and workflow customizations that fit the needs of a modern email experience.

## Installation

To build and run the project locally:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
./build/src/Gui/trojita
```

More information about the project is available from the KDE homepage:

http://apps.kde.org/trojita/

## Customizing your experience

Plasma Mail can be customized in a few practical ways:

### Configuration structure

```yaml
account:
  provider: "gmail"
  maildir: "~/Mail"
  folders:
    - "INBOX"
    - "Archive"
    - "Sent"

ui:
  compact_mode: true
  accent: "plasma-blue"
  threading: true

notifications:
  enabled: true
  desktop_alerts: true
```

### Common customization areas

- Account setup and folder sync
- Reader layout and thread grouping
- Compact/comfortable density mode
- Notification and message alert preferences
- Search and sorting behavior
- Mailbox organization and folder visibility

### Features to explore from the inspiration set

#### KDE / Trojitá-inspired features

- IMAP-first account model and accurate folder synchronization
- Desktop-native message navigation and protocol reliability
- Strong focus on low-footprint local operations and clear account state
- Clean separation between list, preview, and account navigation panes

#### Aqua Mail-inspired features

- Quick actions for archive, move, mark read, and reply without leaving the list
- Compact reading modes for fast triage and low-friction scanning
- Clear search and filter workflows for inbox triage
- Simplified message grouping that prioritizes readability and cadence

#### The Bat!-inspired features

- Power-user bulk actions for flagging, moving, and organizing mail
- Keyboard-driven workflows for message management and filters
- Folder and rule organization patterns that preserve high information density
- Flexible view modes for users who want a classic desktop-mail workflow

#### Kontact-inspired features

- KDE and Plasma integration patterns for notifications and desktop behavior
- User workflows that fit naturally with contact and calendar-oriented mail use
- Cross-application consistency in actions, state, and desktop polish
- Better system integration for account state, reminders, and mail actions

### Customization blueprint

```yaml
ui:
  density: "compact"
  accent: "plasma-blue"
  theme: "plasma" 
  sidebar: "visible"
  preview_pane: true
  threading: true
  focus_mode: false

mail:
  default_reply_mode: "reply-all"
  mark_read_on_open: true
  archive_after_reply: false
  show_sender_avatar: true

notifications:
  enabled: true
  desktop_toasts: true
  sound: false
  sync_status_alerts: true

workflow:
  quick_actions: ["reply", "archive", "flag", "move"]
  keyboard_shortcuts: true
  folder_order: ["Inbox", "Sent", "Archive", "Trash"]
```

These settings make the app feel similar to a configurable Plasma Opus-style product layer: a clean default interface, optional denser modes, themeable UX, and feature toggles that can evolve with the app without changing the underlying architecture.

### Local workflow

1. Configure an IMAP account
2. Refresh folder listings and sync messages
3. Use the message list and viewer for daily reading
4. Extend the interface or workflow for desktop-specific behavior

## Project layout

This repository follows a standard KDE-style application structure:

- `src/` — application source and GUI code
- `po/` — translation resources
- `docs/` — planning, roadmap, and reference material
- `tests/` — test coverage and verification cases
- `cmake/` — build configuration and modules

## Contributing

This project is intended to evolve with input from both core mail-client workflows and Plasma desktop-specific improvements. Feature work should stay grounded in maintainability, account compatibility, and user experience clarity.
